{
  "step_1": "The decision reason concerns the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed, which occurs when a greater portion of the probability mass is assigned to favorable (higher) outcomes compared to unfavorable (lower) outcomes. This can be assessed from the outcomes and probabilities of the lotteries. For Lottery A: 2400€ with 100% probability — this is a degenerate distribution with no variability, so it has no skewness in the typical sense (no tail). For Lottery B: 2500€ (33%), 2400€ (66%), 0€ (1%). The outcome 0€ is unfavorable, but it has a very low probability (1%). The majority of the probability (99%) is on outcomes that are either equal to or higher than the certain outcome of Lottery A (2400€ and 2500€). Thus, Lottery B has a long tail to the right (positive skew) due to the 2500€ outcome with 33% probability, but also a small left tail (0€). However, the reason defines 'left-skewed' as having greater probability mass on favorable outcomes — which aligns with the idea that more weight is on higher values. In this case, Lottery B assigns 99% of probability to outcomes ≥2400€, while Lottery A assigns 100% to 2400€. So, Lottery B is more left-skewed in the sense that it includes a higher outcome (2500€) with non-negligible probability and only a tiny chance of a loss. Therefore, the reason can be applied: it would prefer Lottery B over Lottery A because it is more left-skewed (greater mass on favorable outcomes).",
  "step_2": "The individual considered that Lottery B does not involve any losses (i.e., no guaranteed loss), and that the probability of receiving 0€ is extremely low (1%). They also note that the potential gain of 100€ (compared to 2400€) is not entirely unlikely. The individual’s justification centers on the absence of guaranteed losses and the low but non-negligible chance of a higher payout (2500€). They do not mention variance, expected value, or risk aversion explicitly. Instead, they focus on the low probability of a loss and the possibility of a slightly higher gain. This suggests that the individual evaluated the distribution of outcomes, particularly the minimal risk of a loss and the presence of a higher payoff, and used this to justify their choice.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason evaluates skewness based on whether more probability is assigned to favorable outcomes. In this case, Lottery B assigns 99% of probability to outcomes ≥2400€, which is more favorable than Lottery A’s 100% at 2400€. Thus, Lottery B is more left-skewed according to the reason’s definition. The individual’s reasoning focuses on the absence of losses (i.e., no mass on very unfavorable outcomes) and the non-negligible chance of a higher payoff. This corresponds to the idea that the lottery has more weight on favorable outcomes. The individual’s justification aligns with the rule of the decision reason: they preferred the lottery with higher probability on favorable outcomes (even if slightly higher), and downplayed the extreme loss due to its low probability. Although the individual does not use the term 'skewness' or explicitly refer to probability distribution shape, their reasoning matches the underlying logic of the reason — favoring the lottery with more mass on favorable outcomes. Therefore, the decision reason was likely used, though implicitly. The match is strong but not perfect due to lack of explicit reference to skewness or distributional shape.",
  "final_assessment_score": 85
}

